# inc-19ay, scenario 2 of 2: a breather whose statblock declares NO dice at
# all, so every die it delivers comes from the age template's Power. Red
# dragons declare A_BREA for 0d12 (lib/mon1.irh:564) and the TM_AGECAT
# templates supply Power +1 (hatchling) through +6 (adult), lib/mon1.irh:1274
# onwards. A fix that floored only the declared dice would give this dragon one
# die at every age, which is the loss this scenario exists to catch.
#
# ONE DRAGON, NOT TWO, and the age is the one the game picks. Two earlier
# shapes of this file do not work and should not be tried again:
#
#   * "Add Template to Creature" cannot reach the dragon. It targets through
#     EffectPrompt's Q_TAR ring (src/Term.cpp:2106), which skips anything the
#     player does not perceive, and a summoned dragon drops an unnaturally
#     shadowed region that cuts the player's sight to one square. Measured:
#     @until spent its whole 200-press budget with the cursor still on the
#     player.
#   * A second summoned red dragon is the same age as the first, so it adds no
#     second Power. Measured on seed 1: four breaths, all at Power 5.
#
# Monster Summoning gives the dragon its age itself -- MonsterPrompt has no age
# picker, but Debug.cpp:670 runs EV_BIRTH and Initialize, and the dragon
# arrives templated. On seed 1 it arrives a young adult, Power 5, and breathes
# 0 + 2*5 = 10 dice of d12.
#
# Six squares east: near enough to be in sight when it is summoned, far enough
# that placing a gargantuan dragon does not shove the player out of his own
# square. At three squares it does.
@include chargen.keys

# chargen.keys ends in play mode, so the first key here is a game command and
# NOT a prompt dismissal: an ESC opens the System Menu (src/Player.cpp:845) and
# every later keystroke then reads the wrong screen. Go straight to wizard
# mode, the way kobold-horn-hostile.keys does. The first wizard entry asks for
# confirmation; enable Cheat Death (the first switch) so a level-one character
# can give every summoned monster a turn.
w
y
@choose "Wizard Mode Switches"
SPACE
ESC

w
@choose "Monster Summoning"
@choose "Dragon"
@choose "red dragon"
RIGHT*6
ENTER
@dump:dragon-summoned

# NO "All Monster Aggravate" HERE. That wizard entry crashes the game:
# src/Debug.cpp:1005 passes myHandle -- an object handle -- as the rID
# argument of GainPermStati, Thing::GainPermStati asserts theGame->Get(eID)
# on it (src/Status.cpp:188), and the session then segfaults. A monster the
# wizard menu summons is hostile already, so the aggravate was never needed.

# Each dot gives the monsters a turn. In Player::Death, n declines death
# and restores HP; y accepts death and can prevent the next breath.
.
@while "Die?" n
@dump:wait01
.
@while "Die?" n
@dump:wait02
.
@while "Die?" n
@dump:wait03
.
@while "Die?" n
@dump:wait04
.
@while "Die?" n
@dump:wait05
.
@while "Die?" n
@dump:wait06
.
@while "Die?" n
@dump:wait07
.
@while "Die?" n
@dump:wait08
.
@while "Die?" n
@dump:wait09
.
@while "Die?" n
@dump:wait10
.
@while "Die?" n
@dump:wait11
.
@while "Die?" n
@dump:wait12
.
@while "Die?" n
@dump:wait13
.
@while "Die?" n
@dump:wait14
.
@while "Die?" n
@dump:wait15
.
@while "Die?" n
@dump:wait16
.
@while "Die?" n
@dump:wait17
.
@while "Die?" n
@dump:wait18
.
@while "Die?" n
@dump:wait19
.
@while "Die?" n
@dump:wait20

@quit
